{"cluster_id": "C-0154", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B035-036"], "canonical_claim": "An inline-only CA part could reach `publishStaging` with nonempty staged entries and `st.build == nullptr`, causing a `LOGICAL_ERROR`; the inline write path now calls `buildFor(route, st)` so an all-inline part has a `Build`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["utils/ca-soak/scenarios/BACKLOG.md#L1510-L1551"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0155", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B036-059", "B042-044"], "canonical_claim": "The worklog observed incomplete LIST answers for the same ref-log prefix: `0x1430c` and `0x1430d` were present by `HEAD` but omitted from a fold scan after `0x1430e`, and deletion, append ordering, and not-yet-landed writes were excluded for that occurrence.; RustFS has demonstrably returned incomplete LIST results, while AWS S3 was untested in the cited investigation; correctness cannot rely on assumed store LIST behavior.", "issue_ids": ["T12"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/draft-fixes-20260726.md#p4-chain", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/CURRENT.md#L807-L863"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0156", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B044-029"], "canonical_claim": "HEAD-first deduplication makes an existing content key observably cheaper to write, allowing an inserter to test whether candidate content already exists anywhere in a pool shared across namespaces.", "issue_ids": ["SEC-2"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L968-L975"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0157", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-120"], "canonical_claim": "During the S3-native-staging end-to-end validation on RustFS, staged blobs were found to be stored without the 256-byte `CABL` envelope header \u2014 a data-corrupting bug invisible to 17 in-memory unit tests \u2014 fixed by dropping `logical_size`/`logical_hash` from the staging path and writing the header directly into the staging object ahead of hashing; a related fix introduced fresh-tag resurrect to close an `INV_NO_RETURN` self-condemn hole this staging change exposed.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-11-unattended-cas-campaign.md#L138-L145"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0158", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-225"], "canonical_claim": "`WriteSettings.h` grew six CA-driven fields including `shared_ptr<const S3::Client> s3_client_override`, and the struct's defaulted `operator==` now compares that shared_ptr by identity; because `WriteSettings` is copied on hot write paths, this adds atomic refcount traffic and a widened recompile blast radius, with surprising equality semantics on the client pointer.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/05-integration.md#8-weak-places"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0159", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-228", "B013-052"], "canonical_claim": "412/`PreconditionFailed` detection is duplicated across 3-4 sites (`Client::RetryStrategy`, `removeObjectIfTokenMatches`, `copyObjectConditional`) via `exc.getExceptionName()==\"PreconditionFailed\" || message.find(\"PreconditionFailed\")`, so a store with a differently-worded error body could silently misclassify a precondition failure; the fix is a shared `S3Exception::isPreconditionFailed()` helper.; The shared S3 412 policy is consolidated into `S3::isPreconditionFailedError` and `S3Exception::isPreconditionFailed`, checking the HTTP response code first and canonical exception name/message as fallback.", "issue_ids": ["A-D1", "A\u00a713.2#1"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-12-cas-stabilization-cleanup.md#L2117-L2229", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/05-integration.md#8-weak-places", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-12-cas-stabilization-cleanup-design.md#L169-L176"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0160", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-248"], "canonical_claim": "Risk register #14 (Med-Low, confirmed): `CasXXH3.h:25` includes xxHash via a 6-level relative path (no CMake target) specifically to dodge lz4's bundled copy, which breaks silently on file moves or include-order changes. Suggested action: add a `ch_contrib::xxhash` include dir.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L555"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0161", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-323"], "canonical_claim": "B92 requires an adopted leaf to carry its real `blob_size` rather than a placeholder or zero value.", "issue_ids": ["B92"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/06-tests.md#3-regression-pin-archaeology-bug-ids-invariant-encoded"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0162", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B001-004", "B004-004", "B011-043"], "canonical_claim": "A blob is identified by the pair BlobRef = (algo, digest), never by a bare digest, because multiple 16-byte hash algorithms can coexist. A blob is identified by the pair `BlobHashAlgo` and `BlobDigest`; a bare digest is not an identity because pools may mix `CityHash128`, `XXH3_128`, and `Sha256`. The immutable blob identity in a mixed-algorithm pool is the pair `BlobRef{algo, digest}`; a bare digest is not a blob identity and equal digest values under different algorithms name different objects.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#blobs", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L219-L223", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-11-cas-mixed-algo-pools-design.md#real-invariant"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0163", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B001-005", "B040-167"], "canonical_claim": "Blob safety requires a fresh in-body incarnation_tag for every upload, a backend token, and exact-token deletion; resurrecting a condemned blob re-uploads from the writer's source with a fresh tag.; The v3 cas_blob tag is a fresh random u128 per upload attempt and is the exact-token delete primitive; bld attributes newborn debris to the writer build", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/codecs_proposal_v3.md#blob-envelope", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L219-L223"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0164", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B001-037"], "canonical_claim": "A BlobDigest is a fixed 32-byte tail-zero value whose meaningful length is 16 bytes for ch128 and xxh3 and 32 bytes for sha256.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/02-store-core.md#L90-L92"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0165", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B001-039"], "canonical_claim": "The backend contract requires deleteExact to remove only the matching incarnation and requires TOKEN\u27f9CONTENT so a token match implies unchanged bytes for manifest cache reuse.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/02-store-core.md#L164-L186"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0166", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B001-056", "B002-035", "B002-091", "B043-021"], "canonical_claim": "MetaState::Clean means a blob is referenceable and MetaState::Condemned means GC has marked its in-degree zero; GC writes Condemned before deleting the body, and absent metadata is treated as live. GC's writer freshness marker is a per-hash `.meta` object with `Clean` and `Condemned` states; a writer that reads `Condemned` aborts reuse and re-uploads from source. Per-hash freshness metadata uses magic `CAMT` and two states: `Clean = 0` means referenceable body present, and `Condemned = 1` means GC marked zero in-degree while the body remains present for possible CAS resurrection.; Freshness metadata is advisory: writers may point-read per-hash `.meta`, but reads do not consult it, GC deletes the blob body first, and an absent meta is equivalent to `Clean`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/03-writer-protocol.md#L109-L129", "docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#three-cursor-merge", "docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#per-hash-meta", "docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-edge-before-observe"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0167", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B001-057"], "canonical_claim": "A part write proceeds as writeFile() \u00d7 N followed by precommitAdd(), putBlob() \u00d7 N, and promote().", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/03-writer-protocol.md#L163-L172"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0168", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B001-059"], "canonical_claim": "stageManifest writes the manifest with putIfAbsentStream before precommitAdd, and no blob body HEAD is required before the precommit CAS.", "issue_ids": ["B188", "B190"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/03-writer-protocol.md#L191-L218"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0169", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B001-060", "B004-013"], "canonical_claim": "The revival invariant forbids reading a condemned or vanished blob to revive it; revival re-uploads the writer's own source bytes with a fresh incarnation. A condemned blob is never adopted or server-side-copied; it is recreated from the writer's own source bytes under a fresh `incarnation_tag`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/03-writer-protocol.md#L221-L240", "docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#safety-invariants"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0170", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B002-037", "B005-030"], "canonical_claim": "`deleteExact(blobKey, token)` treats `Deleted`, `TokenMismatch`, and `NotFound` as successful terminal outcomes for the corresponding delete attempt. `deleteExact(hash, token)` treats `Deleted`, `TokenMismatch`, and `NotFound` as completed delete outcomes; a token mismatch means a writer recreated a fresh live incarnation, which can be re-condemned by a later round.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#deletes-publish-cas", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence-redesign.md#gc-round-protocol-one-pass"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0171", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B002-083"], "canonical_claim": "The blob envelope has a fixed 256-byte header with a 94-byte core containing `magic`, `writer_version`, `compatibility_version`, `hash_algo`, `flags`, `header_len`, `logical_size`, `logical_hash`, `domain_id`, `incarnation_tag`, `build_id`, and `header_hash`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#envelope-core"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0172", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B002-084"], "canonical_claim": "The envelope core is never extended; new fields go into the `[94, header_len)` TLV area and `header_len` is padded to an 8-byte multiple with a pool payload offset of 256 bytes.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#envelope-core"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0173", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B002-085"], "canonical_claim": "`compatibility_version > G_BUILD` decodes as `UNKNOWN_FORMAT_VERSION`, bad magic or header hash decodes as `CORRUPTED_DATA`, and unknown critical TLVs decode as `UNKNOWN_FORMAT_VERSION`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#envelope-core"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0174", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-003", "B002-088", "B004-005"], "canonical_claim": "CAS blob hashing is streaming, and `CityHash128` preserves ClickHouse's legacy chunked `CityHash128WithSeed` convention rather than using a one-shot hash for large inputs. Blob identity is the streaming chunked hash of raw payload bytes using 2048-byte `DBMS_DEFAULT_HASHING_BLOCK_SIZE` blocks, not a one-shot hash of the whole payload.; A blob's identity H is a streaming, chunked cityHash128 computed incrementally via HashingWriteBuffer with block size DBMS_DEFAULT_HASHING_BLOCK_SIZE = 2048 as bytes are written, not a one-shot hash of a pre-existing buffer and not read from checksums.txt; the storage key is blobs/<H[:2]>/<H>.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#blobs", "docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#codecs-and-determinism", "docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#blobs"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0175", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B002-086", "B002-087", "B002-090", "B035-078", "B040-079"], "canonical_claim": "A blob hash covers `[header_len, EOF)` payload bytes and excludes the envelope header and incarnation zone. A blob's incarnation zone is excluded from logical_hash, so a new physical incarnation can have a different token without changing the blob id; The envelope's `domain_id`, `incarnation_tag`, and `build_id` are excluded from `logical_hash`, allowing a new physical incarnation without changing the content address. Additionally, The incarnation tag is carried in the object body rather than provider metadata so changing it creates a new backend token without a metadata copy and remains portable across S3, GCS, and Azure. Additionally, Incarnation identity is stored in the object body rather than provider metadata because metadata is write-once and requires copy operations, has an 8 KiB cap, and S3 ETags identify content rather than metadata.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#codecs-and-determinism", "docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#incarnation-in-body", "docs/superpowers/cas/CONSOLIDATION-COVERAGE.md#blocking-gaps", "docs/superpowers/cas/CONSOLIDATION-COVERAGE.md#gap-fill", "docs/superpowers/cas/codecs.md#blob-object-cabl"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0176", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B002-101"], "canonical_claim": "`Cas::Backend` exposes token-aware `get`, `getStream`, `head`, `putIfAbsent`, `putIfAbsentStream`, `putOverwrite`, `casPut`, `deleteExact`, and `list`, plus `supportsListTokens`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#cas-backend"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0177", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B002-103", "B004-010"], "canonical_claim": "`deleteExact(key, token)` removes only the matching incarnation and returns `TokenMismatch` without changing the object when the token differs. `deleteExact(key, token)` deletes only the named object incarnation; `TokenMismatch` leaves the object readable.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#exact-token-deletes", "docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#backend-requirements"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0178", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B002-106"], "canonical_claim": "S3 and Azure use content-derived ETags as incarnation tokens, allowing CA to use the PUT response ETag without a follow-up HEAD; dedup reuse after `PreconditionFailed` still needs a HEAD.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#etag-vs-generation"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0179", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B002-107"], "canonical_claim": "GCS generation tokens are not content-derived, so generation stores disable LIST-token shortcuts and require the probed conditional dialect and token semantics.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#etag-vs-generation"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0180", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B002-110"], "canonical_claim": "Generation-1 format evolution uses `compatibility_version` write-down-to-floor: additive protobuf fields are skipped by old readers, while breaking binary changes require a floor-gated branch.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#write-down-to-floor"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0181", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B002-123", "B003-031", "B051-072"], "canonical_claim": "`getResultObjectETag()` exposes the PUT or multipart-completion ETag after the upload finalize barrier so CA can record an incarnation token without a follow-up HEAD.; `WriteBufferFromFileBase` exposes `getResultObjectETag`, and `WriteBufferFromFileDecorator` forwards it so a CAS writer can record the written incarnation token without a follow-up HEAD request. Additionally, The ETag fix makes `WriteBufferFromS3` return the `PutObject` or `CompleteMultipartUpload` response ETag, eliminating the head-after-put `HEAD`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/07-s3-budget.md#reduction-history", "docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#write-buffer-etag", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/05-integration.md#4-general-infra-additions-class-b-mini-specs"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0182", "topic": "blob protocol contract", "target": "architecture/blob-protocol", "member_ids": ["B002-124"], "canonical_claim": "Native conditional copy uses `If-None-Match: *`; a 412 means the destination already exists and returns `{created=false}`, while disabling native copy fails with `NOT_IMPLEMENTED` instead of doing an unconditional copy.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/05-integration.md#4-general-infra-additions-class-b-mini-specs"]}

